File: WritingWithInk.info,  Node: Advanced multiple conditions,  Next: Logical operators AND and OR,  Up: Conditional Choices

1.7.3.1 Advanced: multiple conditions
.....................................

You can use several logical tests on an option; if you do, _all_ the
tests must all be passed for the option to appear.

  * { not visit_paris }   [Go to Paris] -> visit_paris
  + { visit_paris } { not bored_of_paris }
    [Return to Paris] -> visit_paris


File: WritingWithInk.info,  Node: Logical operators AND and OR,  Next: Advanced knot/stitch labels are actually read counts,  Prev: Advanced multiple conditions,  Up: Conditional Choices

1.7.3.2 Logical operators: AND and OR
.....................................

The above "multiple conditions" are really just conditions with an the
usual programming AND operator.  Ink supports ‘and’ (also written as
‘&&’) and ‘or’ (also written as ‘||’) in the usual way, as well as
brackets.

  * { not (visit_paris or visit_rome) && (visit_london || visit_new_york) } [ Wait. Go where? I'm confused. ] -> visit_someplace

   For non-programmers ‘X and Y’ means both X and Y must be true.  ‘X or
Y’ means either or both.  We don't have a ‘xor’.

   You can also use the standard ‘!’ for ‘not’, though it'll sometimes
confuse the compiler which thinks ‘{!text}’ is a once-only list.  We
recommend using ‘not’ because negated boolean tests are never that
exciting.


File: WritingWithInk.info,  Node: Advanced knot/stitch labels are actually read counts,  Next: Advanced more logic,  Prev: Logical operators AND and OR,  Up: Conditional Choices

1.7.3.3 Advanced: knot/stitch labels are actually read counts
.............................................................

The test:

  * {seen_clue} [Accuse Mr Jefferson]

   is actually testing an _integer_ and not a true/false flag.  A knot
or stitch used this way is actually an integer variable containing the
number of times the content at the address has been seen by the player.

   If it's non-zero, it'll return true in a test like the one above, but
you can also be more specific as well:

  * {seen_clue > 3} [Flat-out arrest Mr Jefferson]


